{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 160,
    "dDurationMs": 3380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thank you to Wren for supporting PBS."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3540,
    "dDurationMs": 4109,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In the world of quantum mechanics, it’s\nno big deal for particles to be in multiple"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 7649,
    "dDurationMs": 4370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "different states at the same time, or to teleport between locations, or to influence each other"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 12019,
    "dDurationMs": 5721,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "faster than light. But somehow, none of this\nstrangeness makes its way to the familiar"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 17740,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "scale of human beings - even though our world\nis made entirely of quantum-weird building"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 22980,
    "dDurationMs": 7250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "blocks. The explanations of this transition\nrange from the mystical influence of the conscious"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 30230,
    "dDurationMs": 6610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mind to the grandiose proposition of multiple\nrealities. But there’s one explanation that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 36840,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "feels as down to earth as the classical world\nthat we’re trying to explain. Let’s see"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 42160,
    "dDurationMs": 2269,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "if it actually makes sense."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 49747,
    "dDurationMs": 4923,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In quantum mechanics, particles don’t have definite properties. Rather they are described"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 54670,
    "dDurationMs": 6049,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by something called the wave function. In fact, a particle is its wave function: a fuzzy"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 60719,
    "dDurationMs": 5759,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "distribution of possible properties that only\nbecome sharply defined in particular circumstances."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 66478,
    "dDurationMs": 4432,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example when we make a measurement of\na particle, the property that we’re measuring"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 70910,
    "dDurationMs": 5270,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "seems to be plucked from the wide range of\npossible values defined by the wave function."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 76180,
    "dDurationMs": 5870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We say that the wave function collapses - it\nappears to shrink to a window whose narrow"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 82050,
    "dDurationMs": 2900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "width is defined by the precision of our measurement."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 84950,
    "dDurationMs": 6270,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A famous example of this is Schrodinger’s cat. A scientist puts a cute kitty in a closed"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 91220,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "box with a radioactive atom attached to a\nvial of poison gas. The atom has a 50-50 chance"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 97300,
    "dDurationMs": 5850,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of decaying, triggering the release of gas\nand so killing the cat. Prior to opening the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 103150,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "box, from the scientist’s perspective the\natom’s wave function exists in what we call"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 108030,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a superposition of states. It is simultaneously\nin a state of decayed and not-decayed. So"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 114430,
    "dDurationMs": 6651,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "then, is the cat also in a superposition of\ndead and alive? Honestly, probably not. At"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 121081,
    "dDurationMs": 5129,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "some point between atom and cat the fuzziness\nof the atom’s wave function collapses into"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 126210,
    "dDurationMs": 9300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "one of the two states. And becomes or. Decayed\nand not decayed becomes decayed OR not, and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 135510,
    "dDurationMs": 5850,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the cat is alive or dead, not alive and dead.\nThe quantum becomes classical at some point"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 141360,
    "dDurationMs": 2790,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "between the subatomic and the macroscopic."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 144150,
    "dDurationMs": 4170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The idea of wave function collapse was first\nproposed by Werner Heisenberg, one of the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 148320,
    "dDurationMs": 5790,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "principle founders of quantum theory. Heisenberg\nand his friend Neils Bohr were convinced that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 154110,
    "dDurationMs": 5410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this wave function collapse was real. It’s\na central part of their Copenhagen interpretation"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 159520,
    "dDurationMs": 6190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of quantum mechanics. But neither physicist\nclaimed to know where or how wave function"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 165710,
    "dDurationMs": 5330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "collapse actually happened. And fair enough,\nbecause it’s confusing. Quantum superpositions"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 171040,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "can involve many quantum particles. So how\nfar can the superposition extend? The atom,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 177360,
    "dDurationMs": 5570,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the radioactive detector, the vial of poison,\nthe cat, the scientist?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 182930,
    "dDurationMs": 5740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Opinions on the matter span all extremes.\nJohn von Neumann and Eugene Wigner thought"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 188670,
    "dDurationMs": 5010,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that wave function collapse happens at the\ninstant of subjective awareness - in other"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 193680,
    "dDurationMs": 5250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "words, they thought that consciousness collapses\nthe wave function. At the opposite end of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 198930,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the spectrum, many physicists believe that\nthe collapse of the wave function is a fiction."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 204290,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example in Hugh Everett’s Many Worlds\ninterpretation, the wave function never collapses,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 210010,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rather lasts forever, splitting into parallel\nrealities. And we have the idea of quantum"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 215610,
    "dDurationMs": 3640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "decoherence, where different parts of the\nwave function simply become unable to interact"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 219250,
    "dDurationMs": 3050,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with each other. We’ve discussed all of\nthese ideas in the past. We also have de Broglie-Bohm"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 222300,
    "dDurationMs": 4490,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pilot wave theory, where particles already\nhave defined properties that are hidden within"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 226790,
    "dDurationMs": 5702,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the wave function. The wave function may collapse, but particles maintain a rigid physical nature."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 232492,
    "dDurationMs": 2978,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We've discussed all of theses ideas in the past. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 235470,
    "dDurationMs": 4220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But today we’re going to look at a different approach to collapsing the wave function."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 239690,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "One that accepts the wave function as the\nfundamental building block of reality, unlike"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 244130,
    "dDurationMs": 6220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pilot wave theory. And one which avoids multiple universes by insisting that collapse does"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 250350,
    "dDurationMs": 7020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "really happen. But it also avoids mystical-seeming\nexplanations like consciousness-induced collapse."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 257370,
    "dDurationMs": 6260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Our story starts in 1986 when three Italian\nphysicists, Giancarlo Ghirardi, Alberto Rimini,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 263630,
    "dDurationMs": 6170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and Tullio Weber published a paper that outlined\nwhat was to become known as GRW theory - the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 269800,
    "dDurationMs": 6530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "first in a new class of alternate quantum\ntheories called “objective collapse theories."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 276330,
    "dDurationMs": 4700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In objective collapse theories, wave functions\nare real, physical entities that literally"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 281030,
    "dDurationMs": 4441,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "collapse when they’re measured. But the\ncollapse has nothing to do with a conscious"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 285471,
    "dDurationMs": 5879,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "observer or any subjective explanation.\nThe wave function and the collapse are completely"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 291350,
    "dDurationMs": 3590,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "real, completely objective — hence the name."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 294940,
    "dDurationMs": 5710,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To understand how objective collapse theories\nwork, we need just a little more quantum mechanics."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 300650,
    "dDurationMs": 4670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The behavior of the wave function is described\nby the Schrodinger equation, which tracks"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 305320,
    "dDurationMs": 3750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "its evolution through space and over time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 309070,
    "dDurationMs": 5180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As we’ve discussed previously, the Schrodinger\nequation is a linear equation - which just"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 314250,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "means that if you add together solutions to this equation, the result is also a solution"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 320570,
    "dDurationMs": 5390,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to the equation. This is part of what makes\nsuperpositions possible - it allows different"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 325960,
    "dDurationMs": 3929,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "parts of the wave function corresponding to\ndifferent possible measurement results to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 329889,
    "dDurationMs": 4891,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "evolve independently of each other. Another thing about the Schrodinger equation is that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 334780,
    "dDurationMs": 5301,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it’s time-reversal-symmetric. Running it\nforwards generates future states, but running"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 340081,
    "dDurationMs": 4649,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it backwards lets you perfectly recover the\npast states."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 344730,
    "dDurationMs": 4159,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When wave function collapse happens, different\nparts of the wave function interact with each"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 348889,
    "dDurationMs": 6791,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "other instantaneously and non-locally and\nnon-reversibly. This means that wave function"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 355680,
    "dDurationMs": 5610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "collapse is a non-linear process and it’s\nnon-reversible. It’s not part of the math"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 361290,
    "dDurationMs": 2730,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the Schrodinger equation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 364020,
    "dDurationMs": 6230,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So to model the effect of wave function collapse,\nGhirardi, Rimini, and Weber added a non-linear"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 370250,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "term to the Schrodinger that could cause this\nwave function collapse in just the right way"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 376050,
    "dDurationMs": 6460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to explain why subatomic systems could be\nquantum but large systems were always classical."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 382510,
    "dDurationMs": 6920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Think of this non-linear action as a rare\nand random hit that the wave function takes"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 389430,
    "dDurationMs": 5890,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at a particular location. That hit causes\nit to collapse to a particular value. And"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 395320,
    "dDurationMs": 4430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "when it collapses, it immediately collapses\nall parts of the wave function that it’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 399750,
    "dDurationMs": 1920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "connected to."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 401670,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The key to making this work is that these\nhits are very rare. It’s incredibly unlikely"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 407070,
    "dDurationMs": 6670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that a single isolated quantum particle will\nundergo collapse during the course of an experiment."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 413740,
    "dDurationMs": 5149,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the more particles you add, the more likely\nthat one of them experiences collapse, and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 418889,
    "dDurationMs": 5971,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that single single event collapses the wave\nfunction of the entire system. Any attempt"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 424860,
    "dDurationMs": 5660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to measure an isolated quantum system necessarily means bringing lots of particles into the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 430520,
    "dDurationMs": 5459,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "picture - in the measurement device and in\nyour own brain. With enough particles collapse"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 435979,
    "dDurationMs": 1421,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "becomes inevitable."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 437400,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So this “hitting” mechanism gives a potential explaination for the quantum-classical divide - it"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 443040,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "simply depends on the number of particles\ninvolved. Small things can stay quantum, but"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 447880,
    "dDurationMs": 5569,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the chance of collapse to classicality increases\nwith size, and big things are essentially"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 453449,
    "dDurationMs": 1611,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "never quantum."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 455060,
    "dDurationMs": 8960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "GRW suggested that the collapse rate should\nbe about 10^-16 hits per second per particle."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 464020,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "With this value, a single particle wave function\nremains uncollapsed for around 100 million"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 469780,
    "dDurationMs": 8850,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "years. But if you have Avogadro’s number\nof particles - the 6x10^23-ish of a macroscopic"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 478630,
    "dDurationMs": 4210,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "object, you expect a collapse every 10 nanoseconds or so."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 482840,
    "dDurationMs": 6540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "GRW was a revolutionary theory that inspired\nmany subsequent models, like Continuous Spontaneous"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 489380,
    "dDurationMs": 7780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Localization. In CSL, physicists imagined that the localizing mechanism was a randomly jiggling"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 497160,
    "dDurationMs": 6650,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "field, like the frenetic Brownian motion of\npollen grains floating on water. Matter’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 503810,
    "dDurationMs": 4060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "interaction with this fluctuating field would\ncontinuously collapse the wave function, in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 507870,
    "dDurationMs": 4399,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "contrast to the discrete and violent hits\nof GRW theory."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 512269,
    "dDurationMs": 3921,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But neither of these models really tried to\nexplain what the mechanism actually was. They"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 516190,
    "dDurationMs": 4369,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "just thought there was some mysterious field that interacted with all matter — almost"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 520559,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "like it was a fifth fundamental force. But\nLajos Diósi and later, Roger Penrose, saw"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 526639,
    "dDurationMs": 4630,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "no need for some new fundamental force. They\nthought nature already gave us a perfectly"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 531269,
    "dDurationMs": 4391,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "good source of wave function collapse: gravity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 535660,
    "dDurationMs": 5161,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Gravitational decoherence would simultaneously\nexplain two mysteries of physics. 1. What"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 540821,
    "dDurationMs": 6818,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "causes the transition from quantum to classical?\nAnd 2. Why can’t gravity be quantized like"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 547639,
    "dDurationMs": 6260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the other forces. All of the collapse models\nattempted to answer point 1, but only Diósi"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 553899,
    "dDurationMs": 5321,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and Penrose’s model could answer 2: gravity\ncan’t be quantized because gravity isn’t"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 559220,
    "dDurationMs": 5890,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quantum. They proposed that gravity and the\nthree quantum forces are diametrically opposed."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 565110,
    "dDurationMs": 5330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Quantum mechanics rules when things are small,\nbut add enough mass, and the gravity of the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 570440,
    "dDurationMs": 5810,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "system will cause it to rapidly decohere into\na classical object. Instead of gravity being"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 576250,
    "dDurationMs": 6269,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quantized, Penrose’s theory predicts quantum\nmechanics will be “gravitized.”"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 582519,
    "dDurationMs": 5031,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example, consider a massive object. General\nrelativity says that the mass from that object"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 587550,
    "dDurationMs": 4530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "will warp the space-time around it. But quantum\nmechanics says that this object can be in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 592080,
    "dDurationMs": 4559,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a superposition of two locations, where it\nis both “here” and “there” at the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 596639,
    "dDurationMs": 5260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "same time. Put these two facts together, and\nyou will get a superposition of two different"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 601899,
    "dDurationMs": 6201,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "geometries of space-time. According to Penrose,\nthis isn’t possible. The instability introduces"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 608100,
    "dDurationMs": 5389,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a nonlinear term in the Schrödinger equation,\ncausing the wave function to rapidly and randomly"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 613489,
    "dDurationMs": 5780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "choose to make the object appear either “here”\nor “there,” but not both."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 619269,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Because each of these objective collapse models\ninvolve modifications to the Schrödinger"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 624309,
    "dDurationMs": 5010,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "equation, they are not mere interpretations\nof quantum mechanics — they are distinct"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 629319,
    "dDurationMs": 5460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "theories with unique predictions. This means\nthat, unlike other interpretations we’ve"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 634779,
    "dDurationMs": 4381,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "discussed — for example, Bohmian mechanics\nor Many Worlds — objective collapse models"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 639160,
    "dDurationMs": 6619,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "can actually be tested. Some tests have already ruled out some of the models and placed restrictions"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 645779,
    "dDurationMs": 2740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on the nonlinear parameters of others."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 648519,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Direct tests of collapse models would involve\nputting a macroscopic object in a superposition"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 653759,
    "dDurationMs": 4541,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of being “here” and “there,” then\nmeasuring how long it takes for the superposition"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 658300,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to collapse. This time should be proportional\nto the size of the object. Experiments are"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 664300,
    "dDurationMs": 4399,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "approaching the masses necessary to make such\ndirect measurements, but they’re not quite"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 668699,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "there yet."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 669699,
    "dDurationMs": 4620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Instead, physicists have come up with clever ways to look for other, indirect signs of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 674319,
    "dDurationMs": 6051,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "collapse models. For example, the models imply that a quantum wave function will be"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 680370,
    "dDurationMs": 6069,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "randomly tossed about and jostled by gravity or some other collapsing field. If the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 686439,
    "dDurationMs": 5041,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quantum object happens to be electrically\ncharged, then the constant jiggling and acceleration"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 691480,
    "dDurationMs": 6599,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "caused by this Brownian motion means it will\nemit radiation. Last year, scientists working"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 698079,
    "dDurationMs": 7331,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in Trieste, Italy tried to measure this radiation\neffect. They put an 8-by-8 cm germanium crystal"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 705410,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in a cryostat and carefully measured the amount\nof radiation it emitted."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 710290,
    "dDurationMs": 6130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The predicted effect was so tiny, researchers\nhad to go underground to try to minimize as"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 716420,
    "dDurationMs": 6389,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "many background sources as possible. In the\nGran Sasso laboratory, a main source of noise,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 722809,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cosmic muons, was reduced by a factor of nearly\n1 million. Then, they further shielded the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 728649,
    "dDurationMs": 5620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "crystal with layers of copper and lead. With\nsuch pristine conditions for a high-precision"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 734269,
    "dDurationMs": 4641,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "measurement, the scientists in Trieste were\nable to measure single photons emitted from"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 738910,
    "dDurationMs": 1760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the germanium crystal."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 740670,
    "dDurationMs": 6159,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "After watching the crystal for two months,\nthey had detected a grand total of 576 photons."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 746829,
    "dDurationMs": 7531,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now this isn't enough to confirm or refute the general idea, but the scientists were able to place tight restrictions on"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 754360,
    "dDurationMs": 7110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the value of the free parameters in the\nDiósi-Penrose model. It even ruled out Penrose’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 761470,
    "dDurationMs": 2103,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "original version of this model."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 763573,
    "dDurationMs": 4366,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There are still many candidates for objective\ncollapse models that have not been ruled out"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 767939,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by experiments, so stand by for a jubilant\nconfirmation or a sheepish “never mind”."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 775139,
    "dDurationMs": 5211,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Whatever the case, it’s exciting that there\nare real and accessible experimental paths"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 780350,
    "dDurationMs": 4099,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to investigating one of the biggest unanswered\nquestions in physics. And one that we’ll"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 784449,
    "dDurationMs": 7370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be coming back to. What, in fact, is the quantum\nwave function? And how does this abstract"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 791819,
    "dDurationMs": 7450,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "system of shifting realities give rise to\nour solid, familiar, and singular space time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 799269,
    "dDurationMs": 4641,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thank you to Wren for supporting PBS. Wren\nis a website where you calculate your carbon"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 803910,
    "dDurationMs": 5510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "footprint, then offset it by funding projects\nthat plant trees, protect rainforests, and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 809420,
    "dDurationMs": 4909,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "suck carbon from the sky. By answering a few\nquestions about your lifestyle, you can find"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 814329,
    "dDurationMs": 4980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "out your carbon footprint and how you can\nreduce it. No one can reduce their carbon"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 819309,
    "dDurationMs": 5310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "footprint to zero, so you can offset what\nyou have left after reducing. Subscribers"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 824619,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "receive monthly updates from the tree planting,\nrainforest protection, and carbon removal"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 829499,
    "dDurationMs": 5421,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "projects they support. You get to see the\ntrees you planted, and what your money is"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 834920,
    "dDurationMs": 5099,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "spent on. You can learn more about Wren’s\nclimate projects at Wren.co"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 840019,
    "dDurationMs": 4010,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Before getting to comments, we’d like to\nthank our supporters on Patreon - we couldn’t"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 844029,
    "dDurationMs": 5610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "do this without you. Today though we also want to give an extra special thanks to Ben Dimock"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 849639,
    "dDurationMs": 4981,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "who’s supporting us at the Big Bang Level.\nBen there are many uncertainties in the world"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 854620,
    "dDurationMs": 5300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of physics. Is the wave function objectively\nreal? Or is it a statistical or subjective"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 859920,
    "dDurationMs": 6359,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fiction? Did I measure position precisely\nor was it momentum? Who put my cat in that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 866279,
    "dDurationMs": 5971,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "funny box, and why isn’t he purring any\nmore? But amid all this uncertainty it’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 872250,
    "dDurationMs": 5809,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a powerful thing to know for sure that our\nscience bills will get paid this month. Ben,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 878059,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "thank you for collapsing our wave function\nin the direction of making more space time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 882459,
    "dDurationMs": 5391,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Last time we tackled a question of utmost\ngravity. Literally. How does gravity itself"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 887850,
    "dDurationMs": 4269,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "escape the inescapable gravity of a black\nhole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 892119,
    "dDurationMs": 7700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The BuzzBen asks what happens when gravitational waves pass through black holes. Is there gravitational"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 899819,
    "dDurationMs": 4790,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "lensing? Well that’s exactly right. Gravitational waves have to move along the same fabric of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 904609,
    "dDurationMs": 5140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "spacetime as everything else. After all, they are wiggles in that very fabric. So they get"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 909749,
    "dDurationMs": 4530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "deflected by gravitational fields just like\nanything else. If that gravitational field"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 914279,
    "dDurationMs": 7511,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is made by a black hole then the result depends\non how direct the hit is, and on the relative"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 921790,
    "dDurationMs": 4950,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "size of the black hole and the wave. A gravitational wave whose wavelength is short compared to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 926740,
    "dDurationMs": 4889,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the black hole’s event horizon can be completely\nswallowed, while a larger gravitational wave"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 931629,
    "dDurationMs": 5570,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "will get deflected and partially absorbed. The\nabsorbed energy can go into changing the black"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 937199,
    "dDurationMs": 3940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hole’s velocity, but also can be added to\nthe black hole’s mass. BuzzBen’s also"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 941139,
    "dDurationMs": 4930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "asks whether it’s possible to focus gravitational\nwaves to a single point to create a black"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 946069,
    "dDurationMs": 5170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hole without mass. The answer is again yes.\nJust as it’s possible to create a black"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 951239,
    "dDurationMs": 4910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hole from pure light - a so-called kugelblitz\n- if you could focus enough energy from gravitational"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 956149,
    "dDurationMs": 3201,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "waves into a small enough region of space\nyou could actually make a black hole from"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 959350,
    "dDurationMs": 1630,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "those waves alone."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 960980,
    "dDurationMs": 8139,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Ryan R asks how we can know that a black hole’s\nmass has time to crush down to the singularity,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 969119,
    "dDurationMs": 6520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "given that time dilation slows the event down during the collapse. So this is a confusing point."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 975639,
    "dDurationMs": 5151,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The time dilation that we talk about approaching\nthe black hole event horizon is not from the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 980790,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "point of view of falling matter, but from\nthe point of view of a distant observer. Only"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 985790,
    "dDurationMs": 5719,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the distant observer sees matter approach\na state of frozen time, and you’re right"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 991509,
    "dDurationMs": 4531,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that from that person’s point of view the\nsingularity - in fact the entire event horizon,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 996040,
    "dDurationMs": 5250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "never quite finishes forming. But from the\nperspective of the falling matter it most"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1001290,
    "dDurationMs": 5519,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "definitely forms, and it is definitely squished\n- though whether it’s to a singularity or"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1006809,
    "dDurationMs": 2152,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "not is still not really known."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1008961,
    "dDurationMs": 6068,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "On that note Pesila Ratnayaje asks if an outsider\nobserver sees matter slow and freeze at the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1015029,
    "dDurationMs": 5571,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "event horizon, what happens when the event\nhorizon grows? Does that matter get swallowed"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1020600,
    "dDurationMs": 7980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "up? This is tricky because that the definition of the event horizon is also tricky. In an idealized"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1028580,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "black hole, the event horizon is defined from\nthe perspective of an observer at “infinite"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1033140,
    "dDurationMs": 7809,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "distance” - aka very very far away, which is after all where you wanna be relative to a black hole. The event"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1040949,
    "dDurationMs": 7521,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "horizon is that surface around the black hole\nbelow which no sub-lightspeed object can escape."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1048470,
    "dDurationMs": 6810,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And by escape I mean reach YOU at your “infinite\ndistance”. And it’s the surface from which"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1055280,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "light is infinitely redshifted - sapped of\nall energy - before it reaches that infinite"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1061160,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "distance."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1062160,
    "dDurationMs": 5010,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We observe an object being frozen at the event\nhorizon if the light emitted at the moment"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1067170,
    "dDurationMs": 5960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it crosses the horizon from its perspective\ntakes infinite time to reach us. So to lose"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1073130,
    "dDurationMs": 5540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "an object beneath a growing black hole means\nthat the event horizon envelopes that final"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1078670,
    "dDurationMs": 6767,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "photon, not the object. By some definitions\nof the event horizon that actually never happens."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1085437,
    "dDurationMs": 4103,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And so everything that went into the black hole remains frozen on the horizon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1089540,
    "dDurationMs": 5510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But to understand why were’ really going to need a whole episode."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1095050,
    "dDurationMs": 3900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Chillyman145 asks a related question, but\nnow it’s for black holes that shrink due"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1098950,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to Hawking radiation. This is a lot more speculative. Some physicists think that the Hawking radiation"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1105070,
    "dDurationMs": 5430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "actually carries away the information from\nprevious infalls. And we actually already"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1110500,
    "dDurationMs": 5210,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have an episode on that one. Check out our\nepisode on the black hole information paradox"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1115710,
    "dDurationMs": 1914,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for the full speculation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1117624,
    "dDurationMs": 6536,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Lucas Thomas asks another related question.\nIf we never see anything cross the event horizon,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1124160,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "why do we say information is lost? When people\ntalk about information being lost in a black"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1128720,
    "dDurationMs": 5190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hole, they’re usually referring to the fact\nthat lots of information goes into building a black"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1133910,
    "dDurationMs": 5510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hole, but  in its formation, but in its original formulation Hawking radiation is supposed"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1139420,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to be completely random - information free\n- and so that when a black hole evaporates all"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1144620,
    "dDurationMs": 5921,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the information that went in is gone. And this is the black hole information paradox that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1150541,
    "dDurationMs": 4355,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I mentioned. And I'll refer you to that episode for more answers. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1154896,
    "dDurationMs": 6744,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dannymac63 likens Space Time to a black hole, in which it’s captivating and radiates information"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1161640,
    "dDurationMs": 5690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "constantly. Well thank you dannymac63. But\nremember, we don’t know whether black holes"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1167330,
    "dDurationMs": 5630,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "radiate actual information. It could be that\nthey, and so by your comparison, this show,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1172960,
    "dDurationMs": 4950,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "only suck in information, and radiate nothing\nbut nonsensical noise. Which of the two is"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1177910,
    "dDurationMs": 7370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the case might be relative to the observer.\nFor example Siderite Zackwehdex loves how"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1185280,
    "dDurationMs": 7420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we ask questions a kid would ask then answer\nin a way they can barely comprehend. So…."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1192700,
    "dDurationMs": 4650,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "“Barely” comprehend means you comprehended\na little bit, right? "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1197350,
    "dDurationMs": 1655,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I’ll take it as a win."
    } ]
  } ]
}
